//
// @FR-F-Spec — a width may be written before any radix letter, and the three base markers
// agree about where they are a marker at all.
//
// `x` was accepted into a numeric literal only directly after a leading `0` (`0x1f`),
// while `b` and `o` were accepted ANYWHERE in a digit run.  So the number scanner ate the
// letter in `{10:6b}`, could not parse `6b`, and reported "Problem parsing number" — a
// diagnostic about a literal, pointing at a format spec, for a program that has no literal
// wrong with it.  Every width-and-binary and width-and-octal spec was unspellable, and the
// hex twin one character away worked, so the reader's evidence said the language had the
// feature.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:   the base letter (`b`, `o`, `x`), whether a width precedes it,
//                         whether a `#` marker is asked for, and the position the letter
//                         is in — the spec's radix slot versus a literal's base marker.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:    the value is a small positive integer.  What a base renders is
//                         `a-format-pad-reaches-the-float-renderers.loft`; the question
//                         here is only whether the spec PARSES.
//
// Apart from that file because the two fail through different channels: on the control
// these cells are a compile error, which would stop a shared file before a single assert
// ran and score every assertion cell in it as vacuously firing.
//
// @falsified-at: 5792ff53 — interpret exit 0 -> 1, native exit 0 -> 1, both through the
// REFUSAL channel: seven `Problem parsing number` errors, one per width-before-`b`/`o`
// spelling, all of them before any assert runs.  The ASSERTION channel reads 0 on both
// trees, which is the point of keeping this file separate from the pad guard — there the
// two numbers are the other way round.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal, expectations are equal on both trees.
//   Falsify prints only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of
//   those is clean on both. The native side was not scored in this sweep.

fn main() {
  // ---- a width before a base letter ---------------------------------------------------
  wb = "{10:6b}";
  wo = "{8:5o}";
  wx = "{255:6x}";
  assert(wb == "  1010", "a width before `b`: {wb}");
  assert(wo == "   10", "a width before `o`: {wo}");
  assert(wx == "    ff", "a width before `x`: {wx}");

  // ---- the same width zero-padded, with and without the `#` marker --------------------
  zb = "{10:06b}";
  zo = "{8:05o}";
  assert(zb == "001010", "a zero-padded binary: {zb}");
  assert(zo == "00010", "a zero-padded octal: {zo}");
  mb = "{10:#06b}";
  mo = "{8:#05o}";
  assert(mb == "0b1010", "`0b` precedes the pad: {mb}");
  assert(mo == "0o010", "`0o` precedes the pad: {mo}");

  // ---- the letters still mark a literal's base ----------------------------------------
  // The fix narrows where a marker is recognised; it must not stop recognising one.
  assert(0b1010 == 10, "a binary literal");
  assert(0o17 == 15, "an octal literal");
  assert(0xab == 171, "a hex literal");
  assert(0xAB == 171, "a hex literal in upper case");
  assert(0b1010_1010 == 170, "a grouped binary literal");
  // A hex literal whose digits include `b` and `o`-adjacent letters still scans.
  assert(0xbeef == 48879, "hex digits that spell a base letter");

  println("base letter OK");
}
